energy required is 561 J.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the heat energy required to raise the temperature of the iron sample, we use the formula:  \n**\\( q = m \\cdot c \\cdot \\Delta T \\)**  \nwhere:  \n- \\( m = 25.0 \\, \\text{g} \\) (mass of iron),  \n- \\( c = 0.449 \\, \\text{J/g\u00b0C} \\) (specific heat capacity of iron),  \n- \\( \\Delta T = 75.0\u00b0C - 25.0\u00b0C = 50.0\u00b0C \\) (temperature change).  \n\n**Step 1: Substitute values into the formula**  \n\\[\nq = (25.0 \\, \\text{g}) \\cdot (0.449 \\, \\text{J/g\u00b0C}) \\cdot (50.0 \\, \\text{\u00b0C})\n\\]  \n\n**Step 2: Perform the calculation**  \n\\[\nq = 25.0 \\cdot 0.449 = 11.225 \\, \\text{J/\u00b0C}\n\\]  \n\\[\nq = 11.225 \\cdot 50.0 = 561.25 \\, \\text{J}\n\\]  \n\n**Step 3: Apply significant figures**  \nAll input values have 3 significant figures, so the final answer is rounded to **561 J**.  \n\n**Answer:**  \nThe heat energy required is **561 J**.", "ground_truth_solution": null, "domain": "chemistry", "source": "camelai_chemistry", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "How does the extent of cross-linking affect the thermodynamic properties of a polymer system? Use Monte Carlo simulations to investigate the relationship between the cross-linking density and properties such as the Flory-Huggins interaction parameter, the radius of gyration, and the heat capacity of the polymer system.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out how cross-linking density affects the thermodynamic properties of a polymer system using Monte Carlo simulations. Let me start by recalling what I know about polymers and cross-linking. Cross-linking refers to the formation of covalent bonds between polymer chains, which can significantly alter the material's properties. The more cross-links there are, the more rigid the polymer becomes, right? Like how vulcanized rubber is harder than natural rubber because of sulfur cross-links.\n\nThermodynamic properties in question her