ave a good leaving group (e.g., iodide > bromide) to facilitate the SN2 reaction.\n\n---\n\n### **Mechanism Summary:**\n1. **Alkoxide Formation:**  \n   \\[\n   \\text{R-X}_1 \\xrightarrow{\\text{HO}^-} \\text{R-OH} \\xrightarrow{\\text{NaH}} \\text{RO}^-\n   \\]\n2. **Ether Synthesis via SN2:**  \n   \\[\n   \\text{RO}^- + \\text{R-X}_2 \\rightarrow \\text{R-O-R} + \\text{X}_2^-\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### **Example:**\nTo synthesize **diethyl ether (Et-O-Et)** using ethyl bromide (Et-Br) and ethyl iodide (Et-I):  \n1. Convert Et-Br to ethanol (Et-OH) via hydrolysis.  \n2. Deprotonate Et-OH to ethoxide (EtO\u207b).  \n3. React EtO\u207b with Et-I to form diethyl ether:  \n   \\[\n   \\text{EtO}^- + \\text{Et-I} \\rightarrow \\text{Et-O-Et} + \\text{I}^-\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion:**\nA symmetrical ether can be synthesized from two different alkyl halides **only if they share the same R group** and differ only in the halide leaving group. The Williamson mechanism proceeds via alkoxide formation followed by SN2 displacement. If the alkyl halides have different R groups, the product will inherently be unsymmetrical.", "ground_truth_solution": null, "domain": "chemistry", "source": "camelai_chemistry", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}]